<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    A small formant speech synthesiser: phonemes in, sample buffer out.

    Split out of Pokey/ rather than left beside it. SpeechSource started there on
    the honest argument that it was pure arithmetic with no UI and no audio API,
    which is true and is still true. But it has grown a phoneme inventory, a frame
    generator and a transition model, none of which has anything whatever to do
    with a sound chip, and the csproj next door already says that when a third
    thing wants a shared file the answer is to extract it rather than link it
    again. Same reasoning, applied a level up.

    The split is real, not cosmetic: this project does not reference Pokey and
    knows nothing about it. It renders to a caller-chosen number of levels, so
    "4-bit" is the caller's fact, not this library's. That is what makes it
    testable on its own and what would let it drive anything else later.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      The XML docs ship with the package, and they are a large part of what this library
      is. CS1591 (and CS1573, a partly-documented parameter list) are off because turning
      them on today means 240 warnings on a build that is otherwise clean, which trains
      everyone to ignore the list. Documenting the gaps is a pre-1.0 job, not a reason to
      lose the signal from every other warning.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
    <AssemblyName>Pokey.Speech</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>PokeySharp.Speech</PackageId>
    <Title>PokeySharp.Speech - a formant speech synthesiser</Title>
    <Description>A formant speech synthesiser in pure C#, with no dependencies and no reference to the chip: phonemes or English text in, samples out, at a caller-chosen number of levels - so it suits a 4-bit DAC without knowing about one. Includes letter-to-sound rules, prosody, voice profiles and singing.</Description>
    <PackageTags>speech;synthesis;formant;text-to-speech;retro;audio;atari;8-bit</PackageTags>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
